   "status": "answered",
   "answer": "Region B law does not create a general public-authority power to access clinical information merely because it has been transferred to or stored in Region B. The Records Access Act gives the Records Access Commissioner a conditional power to order a \u201ccomputing provider\u201d to produce records, but only for national-security purposes or the investigation of serious crime. The order can cover records stored by or accessible to that computing provider in Region B or elsewhere, and does not require court authorisation. On the facts supplied, Harrow is a private health-care provider that does not bill an insurer, keeps the referral in its own clinical record, and stores no records on behalf of another person. It is therefore not a \u201ccovered provider\u201d or \u201cdata host\u201d under the Health Records Act. The facts do not establish that Harrow separately provides storage, hosting or computing services\u2014the status that would make it a \u201ccomputing provider\u201d under the Records Access Act. Accordingly, no presently established production-order power against Harrow is shown. If Harrow\u2019s referral portal is legally operated as storage, hosting or computing services provided by Harrow, section 3 would provide a conditional route for an order; the supplied facts do not establish that alternative classification. If such an order were made, it could require production without court authorisation, could prohibit Harrow from notifying the patient or another person, and would not give the patient a right to notification or review. Harrow would not have to decrypt records or produce keys that it does not hold and cannot obtain. Region B law does not itself require approved transfer clauses or supplementary transfer measures, and does not impose a general prohibition that would prevent compliance with such clauses. Whether a compelled disclosure would conflict with, or require supplementation of, Article 18 clauses is a question for the applicable Article 18 law, not an obligation created by either Region B instrument.",
